--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
3. WHY THE FIVE-COLUMN READING DOES NOT FOLLOW
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The benchmark loaded `4x4.xclbin`. This repo has measured that this overlay occupies all four
columns as ONE partition. So each of those five contexts wants a four-column partition, on a
device that hands out four columns in total. Five contexts times a four-column overlay is
twenty column-occupancies on a machine that exposes four. They cannot be one-per-column, and
the ceiling of five therefore cannot be a column count. It is a driver-side context-table
limit, and the fact that it happens to equal the physical column count is a coincidence of two
small integers.

The benchmark's own second half says the same thing. If two contexts each held their own
column they would run concurrently, which is exactly what this repo measured `1x4.xclbin`
doing when it scaled to 3.65x across per-column partitions. Instead, alternating dispatches
between two contexts costs an extra 747.75 microseconds against the same-context baseline.
A large switch penalty is the signature of TIME-SLICING one partition, not of two partitions
running side by side. The driver work's own conclusion -- that multi-stream execution needs
physical column isolation via `1x4.xclbin` -- is the correct reading of its own data and
agrees with this repo. Only the sentence identifying five contexts with five columns does not.

A NOTE THAT STRENGTHENS THE PENALTY ITSELF. The 7.22x figure is a ratio of means over noisy
samples, and the distributions overlap: the same-context maximum, 881.50 us, is above the
cross-context mean of 867.99 us. The minima separate far more cleanly -- 61.10 us against
467.90 us, a factor of 7.7 -- and a minimum is the right statistic for a floor. The penalty is
real and if anything better supported by the minima than by the means.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
4. THE RUN THAT WOULD SETTLE IT
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Repeat the context-scaling benchmark against `1x4.xclbin` instead of `4x4.xclbin`.

  - If the ceiling is still five, it is a driver context-table limit independent of how many
    columns a context occupies, and the column coincidence is closed.
  - If it becomes four, the ceiling tracks PARTITIONS, which matches
    results/multi_partition_yolov8n_5col.log exactly and still does not make it five columns.

Either outcome removes the ambiguity. Neither requires new tooling: the benchmark already
takes the overlay path.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
WHAT THIS DOES NOT SHOW
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
- Nothing here was measured. Both sides are quoted from existing logs, one of them on a branch
  that is not merged into this one, so that quote is reproducible only while local `main`
  exists at that commit.
- This does not dispute the context-switch penalty, the five-context ceiling itself, the
  NTSTATUS code, or the warm-versus-cold allocation floors. Those are measurements and they
  stand. Only the sentence reading the ceiling as five columns is disputed.
- It also does not establish what the fifth physical column IS for. This repo has never driven
  it by any path, and objective A1 in docs/SILICON.md is still open.
